  "query_preview": "..\n\nSoutheast Michigan will remain on the outer fringe of a strong upper \nlevel ridge (~597 DAM at 500M MB) centered over Kentucky/Tennessee \nTuesday-Wednesday. This presents a challenging temperature forecast \nas clouds and convection could impact the maxes, as they have done \nthe past couple of days. High-res models are continuing to struggle \nwith the extent of showers and thunderstorms, and preference was to \ninclude a low chance of showers and thunderstorms tonight and \ntomorrow. A shortwave is tracking through the Midwest this afternoon \nand will round the weak ridge axis in place across western/central \nGreat Lakes tonight. There is a good 850-700 MB Theta-E ridge/deep \nmoisture axis straddling the southern Michigan border, and with \nanother subtle moisture surge late tonight along and south of I-94 \ncorridor. At the very least, would expect some convective debris \nlingering through the morning hours, which raises concern the max \ntemps make not make it into the lower 90s and will end up closer to \n90 degrees. Thus, confidence was not high enough to issue a Heat A \nadvisory for the possible heat indices of 95-100 degrees. Some drying \nand warming in the mid levels is expected tomorrow afternoon, which \nshould serve as some semblance of a cap, though it is still not \ntotally clear if it will hold completely. If a remnant boundary or \nlake breeze is able to get something going, isolated severe storms \nare possible with MLCAPE of 2000+ J/kg advertised and favorable 0-6 \nKM bulk shear of 40+ knots. Both severe wind and hail possible with \nthe high degree of instability and updraft strength. \n\nMeanwhile, a seasonably strong upper level low is tracking through \ncentral Canada and will slowly fill as it tracks through northwest \nOntario late tomorrow. Uncertainty remains on how well it will drive \na surface frontal boundary meandering over the Great Lakes region \nsouth. Upper level disturbance(s) coming out of the Four Corners \nregion look to arrive into the western/central Great Lakes Tuesday \nnight and especially Wednesday. Exact timing will be important for \nsevere chances, and SPC day 3 Outlook continues to highlight a \nSlight Risk for severe storms. Bulk shear will be very high, and \nthere is a legit chance for convection to develop Tuesday night into \nWednesday morning, once again impacting maxes and severe chances. \nThere still is not enough of a southward push to the front for \nThursday, so a renewed round of showers and thunderstorms remains \npossible as the ridge axis builds back over the Mississippi River \nValley to end the workweek.",
